{"natural_language_statement":"Let $\\sigma(n)$ denote the sum of the divisors of $n$ . Then $f(768) - f(384) = \\frac{\\sigma(768)}{768} - \\frac{\\sigma(384)}{384} = \\frac{(1+\\dots+2^8)(1+3)}{768} - \\frac{(1+\\dots+2^7)(1+3)}{384} = \\frac{511 \\cdot 4}{768} - \\frac{255 \\cdot 4}{384} = \\frac{4}{768} = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{192}}.$","answer":"\\frac{1}{192}","tags":null,"formal_statement":"theorem lean_workbook_plus_12768 :\n  ((∑ k in (Nat.divisors 768), k) / 768 - (∑ k in (Nat.divisors 384), k) / 384) = 1 / 192   :=  by sorry","split":"lean_workbook_plus","proof":["-- The key insight is that `768` and `384` are both powers of `2`, so we can use the formula\n-- for geometric series to sum the divisors. `norm_cast` is used to normalize the numerical\n-- expressions and perform the division.\n\nsimp only [Nat.divisors, Finset.sum_range_succ, Finset.sum_range_zero, zero_add, add_zero]\nnorm_cast","rw [Finset.sum_eq_multiset_sum]\nrw [Finset.sum_eq_multiset_sum]\nrfl","conv_lhs => rw [← Nat.mod_add_div 768 2]","calc (∑ k in Nat.divisors 768, k) / 768 - (∑ k in Nat.divisors 384, k) / 384","refine' Eq.symm _\ncongr 1","calc ((∑ k in (Nat.divisors 768), k) / 768 - (∑ k in (Nat.divisors 384), k) / 384)","unfold divisors\ntrivial","simp only [Nat.divisors]\nrfl"]}
